Step by Step Implementation of a New Operator
#############################################

new_unary.sh
************
If you are creating a new unary operator, it may be easier to use
tools/new_unary.sh.

NEW, OTHER
**********
We use NEW for the new operator's enum value
and OTHER for an already implemented similar operator's enum value.

Tools
*****
The instructions below assume that you have sourced the tools/tools_path.sh
file so that the tools directory is in your path.
For example, check_all.sh and git_commit.sh (referenced below) are in
the tools directory.

Sorting
*******
Let *nb* and *ne* be two positive integers.
If BEGIN\ _SORT_THIS_LINE_PLUS_\ *nb* appears in a source file,
the check_sort.sh tool (which is run during check_all.sh)
will automatically sort source code lines beginning
*nb* lines after the text.  This sorting will end *ne* lines before text
END\ _SORT_THIS_LINE_MINUS_\ *ne* .

NEW_op.cpp TensorType
*********************
The file NEW_op.cpp will specify how forward_par, forward_var, forward_der
and reverse_der functions are calculated for
TensorType equal to at:Tensor and adten_t.
If one of the necessary operations is not yet available for adten_t arguments,
you can set the corresponding function (e.g. reverse_der)
as not yet implemented for adten_t arguments.
For the reverse_der function , this is accomplished by changing
{xrst_code cpp}
    template void NEW_op_t<adten_t>::reverse_der(
        size_t                       op_index    ,
        const agraph_t&              agraph      ,
        const vector<at::Tensor>&    con_vec     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       par_all     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       var_all     ,
        vector<adten_t>&             rev_der
    ) const;
{xrst_code}
to
{xrst_code cpp}
    template <> void NEW_op_t<adten_t>::reverse_der(
        size_t                       op_index    ,
        const agraph_t&              agraph      ,
        const vector<at::Tensor>&    con_vec     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       par_all     ,
        const vector<adten_t>&       var_all     ,
        vector<adten_t>&             rev_der
    ) const {
        // TODO: Change this function to use the TensorType implementation above
        // once the following operators have complete adten_t implementations:
        // list_of_needed_adten_operations
        user_assert(false,
        "reverse_der not yet implemented for NEW with adten_t arguments" );
    }
{xrst_code}
where list_of_needed_adten_operations is replaced by the needed operators.
Some operators may require a totally separate implementation for at::Tensor
and adten_t arguments. This will require more source code and more testing
and will be harder to maintain.

Step 1: Create NEW operator
***************************

Branch
======
Create a new git branch called NEW.

include/ad_tensor/dev/op_enum.hpp
=================================
Add NEW to the enum values in this file;
see OTHER's enum value in this file for an example.

src/adten/NEW.cpp
=================
Add the adten_t implementation for the NEW operator,
see OTHER's implementation in this file for an example.
This includes deciding and documenting how the operator is stored
in the acyclic graphs.

src/CMakeLists.txt
==================
Add the NEW operator's entry in this file just below OTHER's entry.
The entry will be automatically sorted during the check_all.sh below
(see Sorting above).

include/ad_tensor/adten.hpp
===========================
Add the prototype for the NEW operator,
see OTHER's prototypes in this file for an example.

#.  Note the user interface for some operators is not an aten_t member function,
    but its implementation is link to the corresponding member function.

#.  Note that you should add two documentation links to src/adten/NEW.cpp.
    One for the user documentation, the other for the developer documentation..

Test and Commit changes
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that NEW.cpp compiles and that its
documentation builds.
Once the errors are fixed, commit the changes using git_commit.sh.

Step 2: Test Function Without Recording
***************************************

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Add an example and test of computing the NEW operator's function values.
See the part of examples/adten/OTHER.cpp that computes an AD function.
Do something similar for NEW
but just compute and check the adten values (without taping and creating
an AD function).

examples/CMakeLists.txt
=======================
Add the NEW operator's entry in this file just below OTHER's entry.

Test and Commit changes
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that NEW example compiles, runs, and passes it tests.
This will also check that the documentation links in the NEW example.
Once the errors are fixed, commit the changes using git_commit.sh.

Step 3: Create the NEW Derived Operator
***************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Create this file as a copy of the file src/dev/derive_op/noimplement_op.cpp
where the text ``noimplement`` is replaced by NEW.

include/dev/derive_op.hpp
=========================
Add the NEW operator's entry in this file just below OTHER's entry.

src/dev/enum2derive.cpp
=======================
Add two NEW operator entries in this file just below the
corresponding OTHER entries.

Test and Commit changes
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that
the NEW operator, (derived from dev::base_op) compiles and
that the new enum2derive.cpp compiles.

Step 4: Test Using AD Function for Function Values
**************************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the forward_par and forward_var for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
The function forward_par determines the parameter with index op_index.
During this functions, the parameters with index less that op_index
are known and none of the variables are known.
The function forward_var determines the variable with index op_index.
During this function, all the parameters are known
and the variables with index less that op_index are known.

Test and Commit NEW_op.cpp
==========================
Run check_all.sh to check that the new NEW_op.cpp compiles.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
(Change this example (created in Step 2) to record an AD function
that uses the NEW operator's function values.
This should make it more like the section of examples/adten/OTHER.cpp
that computes and tests function values.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.


Step 5: Test Forward Derivative Evaluation
******************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the forward_der for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
The function forward_der determines the forward derivative with index op_index.
During this function, all the parameters and variables are known
and the forward derivatives with index less that op_index are known.

Test and Commit NEW_op.cpp
==========================
Run check_all.sh to check that the new NEW_op.cpp compiles.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Add an example / test of forward_der for the NEW operator;
see examples/adten/OTHER.cpp.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.

Step 6: Test Reverse Derivative Evaluation
******************************************

src/dev/derive_op/NEW_op.cpp
============================
Implement the reverse_der for the NEW operator
(use src/dev/derive_op/OTHER_op.cpp as an example for how to do this).
On input, the partial of the objective w.r.t. each of the variables
with indices less than or equal op_index are known.
Upon return, the result of the operator with index op_index
is removed (as an argument to the objective)
and its contribution is added to each of the NEW operator arguments.

Test and Commit NEW_op.cpp
==========================
Run check_all.sh to check that the new NEW_op.cpp compiles.

examples/adten/NEW.cpp
======================
Add an example / test of reverse_der for the NEW operator;
see examples/adten/OTHER.cpp.

Test and Commit NEW.cpp
=======================
Run check_all.sh to check that the modified example compiles, runs,
and passes it tests.

{xrst_end new_op}
